    "intro": {
      "type": "centre-note",
      "settings": {
        "color_scheme": "paper",
        "heading_tag": "h1",
        "heading_size": "h2",
        "eyebrow": "The technology",
        "heading": "The mechanism, not the claim.",
        "body": "<p>“Waterproof” is a word anybody can print on a label. This is what is underneath ours, so you can judge it rather than take our word.</p>"
      }
    },
    "membrane": {
      "type": "content-columns",
      "settings": {
        "color_scheme": "paper",
        "layout": "prose",
        "eyebrow": "The membrane",
        "heading": "Porelle® is not a name we invented.",
        "prose": "<p>The waterproof layer is Porelle®, a licensed third-party waterproof-breathable membrane. That distinction matters more than it sounds: a membrane you can look up is a membrane someone else has to stand behind. Water cannot get through it; vapour from the foot can. It is PFOA free.</p><p>Brands that do not name their membrane are asking you to trust an unnamed laminate. We would rather you checked ours.</p>",
        "lede": "<p>The waterproof layer is Porelle®, a licensed third-party waterproof-breathable membrane. That distinction matters more than it sounds: a membrane you can look up is a membrane someone else has to stand behind. Water cannot get through it; vapour from the foot can. It is PFOA free.</p><p>Brands that do not name their membrane are asking you to trust an unnamed laminate. We would rather you checked ours.</p><p>The principle is not new and is not proprietary to us. A waterproof-breathable membrane works because liquid water and water vapour are very different sizes. The pores are far too small for a droplet to pass through and comfortably large for a vapour molecule. That asymmetry is the entire technology, and it has been in outdoor clothing for decades.</p>"
      }
    },
    "layers": {
      "type": "content-columns",
      "settings": {
        "color_scheme": "wash",
        "layout": "list",
        "numbered": false,
        "heading": "Three layers, and what each one is for",
        "footnote": "The middle layer is the one doing the waterproofing, and it is the one you never touch. That is deliberate: a membrane exposed to abrasion is a membrane with a short life."
      },
      "blocks": {
        "i1": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Inner layer",
            "body": "<p>Next to the skin.</p><p>A soft knitted lining that moves sweat off the foot and stops the membrane sitting against the skin.</p>"
          }
        },
        "i2": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Porelle® membrane",
            "body": "<p>The waterproof barrier.</p><p>A licensed third-party laminate. Water cannot get in; vapour from the foot can get out.</p>"
          }
        },
        "i3": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Outer layer",
            "body": "<p>Wear surface.</p><p>The knitted face that takes abrasion inside a boot or shoe, and holds the sock's shape when it is not being worn.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "i1",
        "i2",
        "i3"
      ]
    },
    "testing": {
      "type": "centre-note",
      "settings": {
        "color_scheme": "paper",
        "heading_size": "h3",
        "eyebrow": "How this is tested",
        "heading": "We publish no test figures yet.",
        "body": "<p>Independent test data is the only kind worth printing, and we do not have it yet. Rather than quote a hydrostatic head or a breathability rating we cannot evidence, we name the membrane and let you look it up.</p>",
        "footnote": "<p>For context: hydrostatic head measures the water pressure a fabric resists before it lets water through, and breathability is usually given as grams of vapour passed per square metre in twenty-four hours. Those are the two numbers to ask any brand in this category for.</p>"
      }
    },
